## hi-china | Ancient China | history | 0 | - | hi-anc
story 4 | The first emperor was buried with an army of clay soldiers
- Qin Shi Huang unified China in 221 BC and standardised writing, weights, coins and even axle widths.
- His tomb complex holds about 8,000 life-size terracotta warriors, each face different.
- *Farmers digging a well found them in 1974. His main burial chamber is still unexcavated.*
> China invented paper, printing, gunpowder and the compass

quiz 3 | Which was NOT one of the "Four Great Inventions" of ancient China?
* The telescope
o Paper
o Gunpowder
o The compass
= The four: papermaking, printing, gunpowder and the compass.
> The Great Wall isn't one wall

myth 4 F | The Great Wall of China is easily visible from space with the naked eye.
= It's long but only a few metres wide, and similar in colour to its surroundings.
= Astronauts, including Chinese astronaut Yang Liwei, have said they couldn't see it from orbit.
> Next: the Islamic Golden Age

## hi-islam | The Islamic Golden Age | history | 0 | - | hi-china
story 4 | Ibn al-Haytham did experiments on light around 1020
- He showed vision works by light entering the eye, not rays leaving it.
- His Book of Optics used controlled experiments, including the camera obscura.
- *He's often called one of the first true experimental scientists.*
> Our numbers came via Arabic scholars from India

explain 3 | Arabic numerals are Indian
- The digits 0–9 and place value developed in India.
- Arabic mathematicians, like al-Khwarizmi, adopted and spread them.
- *Europeans called them Arabic because that's who they learned them from.*
> Words borrowed from Arabic

quiz 3 | Which English word comes from Arabic?
* Algebra
o Geometry
o Calculus
o Theorem
= From "al-jabr" in al-Khwarizmi's book title: "restoring" or "reunion of broken parts".
= Also: algorithm, alchemy, zero, sugar, coffee.
> Next: the Mongols

## hi-mongol | The Mongol Empire | history | 0 | - | hi-islam
explain 4 | The largest contiguous land empire in history
- At its height in the late 1200s, it stretched from Korea to Hungary: about 24 million km².
- Its postal relay system, the Yam, carried messages up to about 300 km a day.
- *It linked East and West, carrying trade, ideas and, later, the Black Death.*
> Genghis Khan's descendants

story 4 | About 0.5% of men worldwide share one Y chromosome lineage
- A 2003 study found a Y-chromosome lineage in about 8% of men across a large region of Asia.
- The researchers suggested it spread from Genghis Khan and his male relatives.
- *That's an estimated 16 million men.* The link to Genghis himself is a hypothesis, not proven.
> Next: the age of exploration

## hi-explore | Age of exploration | history | 0 | - | hi-mongol
story 4 | Magellan didn't sail around the world
- His expedition left Spain in 1519 with five ships and about 270 men.
- Magellan was killed in the Philippines in 1521.
- *Only one ship and 18 men made it back in 1522, led by Juan Sebastián Elcano.*
> Longitude was a deadly problem

story 5 | John Harrison and the longitude prize
- Ships could find latitude from the sun, but longitude needed accurate time at sea.
- 1714: Parliament offered £20,000 for a solution. Carpenter John Harrison spent decades building sea clocks.
- *His H4 watch passed sea trials in the 1760s.* He had to fight the Board of Longitude for his money.
> The Columbian exchange changed diets

explain 3 | The Columbian Exchange
- Tomatoes, potatoes, maize, chocolate and chillies went from the Americas to the rest of the world.
- Wheat, horses, cattle and diseases like smallpox went the other way.
- *Italian food had no tomatoes before the 1500s.*
> Next: revolutions

## hi-rev | Age of revolutions | history | 0 | - | hi-explore
explain 3 | The French Revolution invented the metric system
- 1790s: revolutionaries wanted rational, universal units.
- The metre was defined as one ten-millionth of the distance from the North Pole to the equator.
- *They also tried a decimal clock with 10-hour days. It didn't catch on.*
> The guillotine was meant to be humane

story 3 | The guillotine was a reform
- Dr Joseph-Ignace Guillotin proposed a single, quick method of execution for all classes, replacing torture and botched beheadings.
- He opposed the death penalty himself.
- *The device was named after him, to his family's dismay.*
> The Haitian Revolution

story 5 | Haiti: the only successful large-scale slave revolt to found a nation
- 1791–1804: enslaved people in Saint-Domingue rose up, led by figures including Toussaint Louverture.
- They defeated French, Spanish and British forces.
- *Haiti declared independence in 1804.* France later forced it to pay a crippling indemnity for "lost property".
> Next: the Victorians

## hi-vict | The Victorian era | history | 0 | - | hi-rev
story 4 | John Snow stopped a cholera outbreak by removing a pump handle
- 1854: cholera killed hundreds around Broad Street, London.
- Snow mapped the deaths and traced them to one water pump. The handle was removed.
- *The founding story of epidemiology, decades before germ theory was accepted.*
> The Great Stink

story 4 | The Great Stink of 1858
- A hot summer turned the sewage-filled Thames so foul that Parliament struggled to sit.
- Joseph Bazalgette's sewer system followed, much of it still in use.
- *He made pipes larger than needed, anticipating growth. That foresight saved London later.*
> Ada Lovelace's notes

story 5 | Ada Lovelace published what's often called the first computer program
- 1843: translating a paper about Babbage's Analytical Engine, she added notes three times longer than the original.
- Note G set out how the machine could compute Bernoulli numbers.
- *She saw that it could manipulate symbols, even music, not just numbers.*
> Next: votes for women

## hi-vote | Votes for women | history | 0 | - | hi-vict
quiz 3 | Which country first gave women the vote in national elections (1893)?
* New Zealand
o United Kingdom
o United States
o France
= New Zealand in 1893, after a petition signed by nearly a quarter of adult women.
= UK women got equal voting rights in 1928; France in 1944.
> The suffragettes

story 4 | Emily Davison and the 1913 Derby
- Suffragette Emily Wilding Davison stepped in front of the King's horse at the Epsom Derby. She died four days later.
- Historians still debate whether she intended to die or to attach a banner.
- *Her funeral drew huge crowds through London.*
> Next: the 20th century's turning points

## hi-20c | 20th-century turning points | history | 0 | - | hi-vote
story 4 | The 1918 flu killed more people than the First World War
- The pandemic infected roughly a third of the world's population.
- Deaths are estimated at 17–50 million or more, with unusually many young adults.
- *It's called "Spanish flu" because neutral Spain's press reported it freely while warring nations censored news.*
> Partition of India

explain 4 | The Partition of India, 1947
- British India was divided into India and Pakistan along lines drawn in about five weeks.
- Around 10–20 million people were displaced; estimates of deaths range up to around a million or more.
- *The boundary was drawn by a lawyer who had never visited India before.*
> The fall of apartheid

story 4 | Mandela was imprisoned for 27 years
- Nelson Mandela was jailed in 1962 and released in 1990.
- He was elected South Africa's first Black president in 1994, in its first fully democratic election.
- *He shared the 1993 Nobel Peace Prize with F. W. de Klerk.*
> Next: inventions

## hi-inv | Inventions that changed everything | history | 0 | - | hi-20c
story 4 | The shipping container transformed the world economy
- Before 1956, ships were loaded piece by piece by armies of dockworkers.
- Malcom McLean's standard steel boxes cut loading costs by more than 90%.
- *Cheap shipping made global supply chains possible.*
> Refrigeration changed what we eat

explain 3 | Fridges changed diets and cities
- Before refrigeration, food had to be salted, smoked, dried or eaten fast.
- Refrigerated ships brought meat from Australia and Argentina to Britain from the 1880s.
- *Stomach cancer rates fell as people ate fewer salted and smoked foods.*
> The bicycle helped women's freedom

explain 3 | The bicycle as a liberation machine
- In the 1890s cycling craze, bicycles gave women unprecedented independent mobility.
- Susan B. Anthony said the bicycle had "done more to emancipate women than anything else in the world".
- *It also pushed changes in women's clothing.*
> History track part two complete
